## a b s t r a c t
Noble metal nanoparticles (NPs) functionalized MOFs are attractive materials for photocatalytic reactions, and outstanding catalytic performances can be expected, especially when they have intrinsically matched crystal faces between metals and MOFs. This study discovered that the photocatalytic oxidation of aromatic alcohols to aldehydes was enhanced over Au/UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ or ${\sf A u/U i O-}66$ but suppressed over Pt loaded counterparts, whereas the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ was boosted over both two catalysts. In reactions, the conversion of benzyl alcohol was $17.1\%$ over UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $7.6\%$ over UiO-66, and an enhanced conversion was obtained over Au/UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ $(30.5\%)$ and Au/UiO-66 $(24.1\%)$ . However, the conversion was decreased over $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}\left(\Omega\right.}$ $(9.3\%)$ and Pt/UiO-66 $(<1\%)$ . Experimental results revealed strong correlations between Zr-MOFs and loaded metal NPs, as Zr centers promoted the formation of Pt(200) planes that suppressed the production of $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ intermediates to oxidize aromatic alcohols, whereas $\mathsf{P t}(200)$ exhibited no effect on the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction triggered by photo-induced electrons. The findings in this study on constructing noble metal NPs functionalized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs catalysts with specially-matched crystal structures and on distinguishing photocatalytic mechanisms on oxidation of alcohols and reduction of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ would provide valuable information for designer (photo)catalysts based on MOFs and their applications in such as catalysis and environmental remediation.
