## 1. Introduction
Photocatalysis promises a green channel to harvest solar energy for chemical energy, clean chemicals synthesis and environmental remediation, such as aromatic alcohol oxidation and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction [1,2]. Selective oxidation of alcohols to their corresponding aldehydes is a significant functional group transformation in organic synthesis, because aldehydes and their derivatives are indispensable building blocks in producing fine chemicals [3]. Cr (VI) is classified as the carcinogen and mutagen for its acute toxicity and strong carcinogenic property, whereas $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ is a nontoxic and essential trace metal for human health [4]. Conventional photocatalysts such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , ZnO or CdS possess large band gaps or may suffer photo-corrosion, leading to inefficient catalytic performances and difficult practical utilization. Moreover, the non-adjustable structures of these conventional semiconductors limited their further development in the field of photocatalysis [5]. Therefore, it is highly imperative to explore efficient, robust and cost-effective photocatalytic materials as alternatives [6,7].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a fascinating family of porous crystalline materials built from metal ions and polyfunctional organic linkers, possessing excellent properties, such as high specific surface areas, large pore volumes, and structural adaptability and flexibility. These properties confer their popular applications in different fields, including adsorption and separation [8,9], heterogeneous catalysis [6,10–12], drug delivery [13] and sensing [14]. Since the first exploration of MOFs as photocatalysts in 2006 [15], MOFs-based materials have become promising photocatalysts for not only chemical synthesis but also environmental remediation [16]. Some MOFs (MIL-53, UiO-66 and MIL-125) are regarded as semiconductor-like materials, with the metal cluster being as the conduction band and the organic linker as the valence band. Nonetheless, the photocatalytic performances of pure MOFs are limited, thus necessitating further functionalization by such as decoration of linkers or metal centers with sensitizers, semiconductors, and metal nanoparticles (NPs) [6]. Among those decoration strategies, loading of metal NPs is especially important, for instance, Pd $\mathsf{N P s}(3-6\ \mathrm{nm})$ loaded UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibited a highly efficient photocatalytic activity to remove $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and dyes simultaneously [17]; smaller Pd nanoclusters $(<1.2\:\mathrm{nm})$ encapsulated in the cages of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ performed efficiently in the visible light-promoted Suzuki coupling reaction [18], and core-shell Pd nanocubes@ZIF-8 photocatalysts improved the selective photocatalytic conversion of small olefins [19]. Other metal-MOFs composites of Au@ZIF-8 [20], M/MIL-100 $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u}$ , Pd and Pt) [21], Pd/MIL-100 [22], M/MIL-125 ( $\mathrm{\dot{\boldsymbol{M}}=\boldsymbol{P}t}$ , Au and Pd) [23], and $N i/\mathsf{M I L}\ –125–\mathsf{N H_{2}}$ [24] have also been reported as photocatalysts; moreover, an opposite effect of Pt and Au is observed, with $\mathsf{P t/M I L{-}}125\mathrm{{-}N H_{2}}$ boosting formate formation and $A\mathsf{u}/\mathsf{M I L}\ –125–\mathsf{N H_{2}}$ suppressing it [25]. However, the underlying catalytic mechanisms, particularly the opposite effect of Pt and Au for the same reaction, in the aforementioned studies are still ambiguous, and if clarified, the findings will be interesting and important in heterogeneous photocatalysis.
In this study, we developed a new series of $\mathsf{M}/\mathsf{Z r}{\mathsf{-M O F}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u}$ and Pt, ${\mathrm{Zr}}{\mathrm{-MOF}}={\mathrm{UiO}}{\mathrm{-}}66$ and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}},$ ) catalysts via a facile post-synthetic method and utilized them in the photocatalytic oxidation of aromatic alcohols and the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ . It is found that Au promoted the oxidation but Pt suppressed it; on the contrary, both Au and Pt boosted the reduction. Excitingly, the underlying mechanism on these different catalytic effects are clarified on the basis of the experimental results and literature reports, which is the specially-matched crystal faces between metal NPs and ZrMOFs and thus-induced differences in intermediate formation or electron transfer. We expect that our findings will provide valuable information and guidelines for designing novel catalytic materials in practical utilization.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ $(98\%)$ , Poly (diallyldimethylammonium chloride) solution (Mw 200,000–350,000, $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{16}\mathsf{C l N})\mathsf{n}$ , PDDA) and Chloroplatinic acid hexahydrate $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathsf{P t}\geq37.5\%$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Company, China. 1,4-benzendicarboxylic acid, N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF), acetic acid, Sodium borohydride $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ , $98\%$ and NaOH were supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent CO., Ltd., China. Hydrogen Tetrachloroaurate (III) Trihydrate $(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ was obtained from Shanghai Titan Scientific Co., Ltd., China. All chemicals were used as received without further treatment.
## 2.2. Zr-MOFs (UiO-66 and UiO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ )
UiO-66 and UiO- $66\substack{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ were synthesized based on literature with a slight modification [26]. In a typical synthesis of UiO-66, $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ ( $190.3~\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.8\mathrm{mmol}$ ) and 1,4-benzendicarboxylic acid ( $133\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.8\:\mathrm{mmol}$ ) were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide $(81.7\mathrm{mL})$ , and then $4.6\:\mathrm{g}$ of acetic acid $\mathbf{\nabla}^{\prime}80\mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ) were added into the solution with ultrasonic treatment for $20\mathrm{min}$ and continuous stirring for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . After that, the mixed solution was placed into a $150\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The solid products were collected by centrifugation and washed three times with DMF and methanol to remove the residual reactant and exchange the DMF. UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ was synthesized by the same method with $147\mathrm{mg}$ of $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -terephthalic acid instead of $133\mathrm{mg}$ of 1,4-benzendicarboxylic acid.
## 2.3. Au and Pt NPs solution
The branched Au and Pt NPs solution in homogeneous dispersion were prepared according to a previous report with modifications [27]. Typically, $2{\mathrm{mL}}$ of KOH solution $(0.1\mathrm{mol}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) added into $26~\mathrm{mL}$ ${\mathrm{HAuCl}}_{4}{\cdot}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ with a concentration of $3.8\times10^{-3}$ mol $\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . After vigorous stirring of $15\mathrm{min}$ , $0.2{\mathrm{g}}$ of PDDA dissolved into $10\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water was added into the above solution and stirred for another $15\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, 10 mL $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ $(0.35\mathrm{mol}.\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ was added into the mixture drop by drop with a time interval of 3 s and a constant vigorous stirring. Pt NPs solution was synthesized by the same method with $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ instead of ${\mathrm{HAuCl}}_{4}{\cdot}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ . The digital photos of as-prepared Au and Pt nanoparticle suspension solution were shown in Fig. S1.
2.4. M/Zr-MOF (Au/UiO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2},$ Pt/UiO-66- $\cdot N H_{2}$ Au/UiO-66 and Pt/ UiO-66)
M/Zr-MOF were prepared by a facile method. Typically, $250\mathrm{mg}$ of UiO-66 or UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was added into $6.2\mathrm{mL}$ as-prepared Au or Pt solution $(1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ (the actual loading-ratio was about $0.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ determined by EDX). Then the mixture was stirred for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ followed by $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ ultrasonic treatment and the processes were repeated for three times. The resulting mixture was washed by deionized water and ethanol for several times and then dried at $80^{\circ}C$ overnight. The obtained $\mathsf{A u/U i O{–}G6{–}N H_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ , Au/UiO-66 and Pt/UiO-66 samples were marked as AUN, PUN, AU and PU, respectively.
## 2.5. Characterization
The crystalline structures of these samples were examined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using Rigaku Ultima IV with Cu Ka radiation $(\mathrm{k}=0.1542\mathrm{nm})$ ) at $40\mathrm{kV}$ The Energy Dispersive X-Ray Fluoresence Spectrometer (EDX) of the samples were observed by utilizing a JSM-7600F (JEOL Ltd., Japan) with an operating voltage of $30\mathrm{kV}.$ . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image and high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) were obtained by a JEOL JEM-2100 instrument at the accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}$ . Nitrogen adsorption-desorption analysis was conducted using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 at $77\mathrm{K}.$ Each sample was degassed at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $120\mathrm{min}$ prior to analysis. The specific surface areas were measured by the BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) method. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, AXIS UltraDLD, Japan) was employed to determine surface electronic states. All the binding energies were referenced to the C1s peak at $284.8\mathrm{eV}$ of the surface adventitious carbon. The UV– vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) of the samples over a range of $200{-}800\mathrm{nm}$ were recorded by UV-2600 (Shimadzu, Japan) spectrophotometer with a $\mathsf{B a S O}_{4}$ reference.
## 2.6. Electrochemical measurements
Photocurrent measurements and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were carried out on a CHI-760E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua Instrument, Shanghai, China) in a standard three-electrode system. Namely, a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) was used as a reference electrode, a Pt foil as the counter electrode, and the samples as the working electrodes. A $0.5\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{\Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ aqueous solution was used as the electrolyte. The working electrodes were prepared as follows: $10\mathrm{mg}$ of the sample was dispersed in $2{\mathrm{mL}}$ DMF and ultrasonicated for $30\mathrm{min}$ , then $0.1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of colloidal solution was dropped on a $1.5\mathrm{cm}\times1.0\mathrm{cm}$ indium-tin oxide (ITO) glass substrate with an active area of about $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ and allowed to dry at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight. For photocurrent measurements, a 300 W xenon lamp (CELHXF300) served as a light source. Mott-Schottky plots were tested at the same conditions with $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ reference electrode instead of SCE and without light irradiation.
## 2.7. Photocatalytic oxidation of benzyl alcohol
The photocatalytic oxidation of benzyl alcohol was carried out under a 300 W Xenon lamp (CEL-HXF300, a CEL-VisREF filter $(350-780\mathrm{nm})$ , $10\mathrm{cm}$ to the reaction liquid) at ambient temperature $(\sim15^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ . In general, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of the as-prepared photocatalyst was dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of aromatic alcohol/toluene aqueous solution $(0.05\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) contained $0.035\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of naphthalene as the internal standard under constant stirring. The choice of solvent toluene was due to its inertness to oxidation and high solubility for molecular $0_{2}$ . In addition, toluene is more economic than another common solvent benzotrifluoride. Prior to illumination, the solution was kept in dark for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ under stirring to rule out the influence of adsorption. After $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , an equal volume of liquid sample was obtained by a syringe to filter the catalysts with $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter. Then the analytical sample was analyzed by a GC (Agilent 7820) equipped with an FID and the HP-5 column. To evaluate the stability, the spent catalyst was collected after the photocatalytic reaction by centrifugation, washing with ethanol and drying for the next test.
## 2.8. Photocatalytic reduction of $C r(W)$
The photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ was carried out under the same conditions with that of photocatalytic oxidation of aromatic alcohol. Typically, $40\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared photocatalyst was dispersed into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ aqueous solution $\mathrm{50mg\L^{-1}}.$ ) under constant stirring. Prior to illumination, the solution was kept in dark for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ stirring to reach an adsorption–desorption equilibrium. At given time intervals, an equal volume of liquid sample was obtained by a syringe to filter the catalysts with $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter. The $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ content was determined colorimetrically at $540\mathrm{nm}$ using the diphenylcarbazide method with a detection limit of $0.005\mathrm{mg}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ [28].
