## 3.1. Materials characterizations
## 3.2. Photocatalytic oxidation of aromatic alcohol
The selective oxidation of aromatic alcohol was carried out utilizing $0_{2}$ as the oxidant upon $350\mathrm{-}780\mathrm{nm}$ light irradiation and the corresponding results were shown in Table 1. The pristine UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ showed a conversion of $17.1\%$ as to benzyl alcohol, and $\mathsf{A u/U i O{–}G6{–}N H_{2}}$ improved the conversion to $30.5\%$ (Such conversion is comparable to that of CdS/UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [2] and UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -F [37]), the enhanced photocatalytic activity, probably due to the following two reasons: (i) Light irradiation could induce the Localized Surface Plasmon Resonance (LSPR) of Au NPs, providing more light absorption of AUN compared with pure UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ . (ii) The work function of Au is $5.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to a low Fermi level which tends to trap electrons [38,39]. In comparison, the work function of $\mathsf{P t}(5.7\mathrm{eV})$ is higher than that of Au; in principle, this is more beneficial to photo-induced electron transfer than that of Au [38], namely, the oxidation of benzyl alcohol compared with pristine UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . However, the conversion of benzyl alcohol was only
## 3.3. Photocatalytic reduction of $C r(W)$
To understand the catalytic mechanisms over supported Au and Pt NPs on $\mathsf{M}/\mathsf{Z r}{\mathsf{-M O F}}$ , other types of photocatalytic reactions are worth exploring. The experimental results of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction were given in Fig. 3a. No Cr(VI) reduction was detected without light irradiation and the pristine UiO-66 showed negligible reduction. In comparison, after loading of Au and Pt NPs, both of their photocatalytic activities were enhanced about 4.0 times of AU and 5.4 times of PU to that of UiO-66, respectively. UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ exhibited an apparent photocatalytic activity for $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction compared with UiO-66 because of the enlarged light absorption [41]. Similarly, loading of Au and Pt NPs further improved the photocatalytic activity, and PUN performed best for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction among these samples. In the presence of PUN as a typical photocatalyst, the change in the typical temporal spectra evolution of DPC-Cr(VI) complex solutions was presented in Fig. 3b, which was obtained by taking out samples from the reactor at various exposure time intervals. The absorption peak at $540\mathrm{nm}$ which was ascribed to DPC-Cr(VI) complex decreases as the function of light irradiation time. The common ground of M/UiO-66 and $\mathsf{M}/\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ for Cr (VI) photocatalytic reduction is that the efficiency of Pt loading is higher than that of Au loading, and the results for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction were consistent with the previous study that the electron transfer effect on Pt was more efficient than the LSPR effect of Au [21].
## 3.4. Proposed mechanism on the above inverse effect
UV–vis diffuse reflection spectra can reflect the light absorption intensity at different wavelengths. As shown in Fig. 4a and b, the absorption edge of UiO-66 occurred at ca. $320\mathrm{nm}$ , leading to its weak response under $350\mathrm{-}780\mathrm{nm}$ light irradiation. Grafting of amino group $\mathrm{(UiO-66-NH_{2}}^{\cdot}$ ) shifted the absorption edge from 320 to $450\mathrm{nm}$ along with color change from white to yellow [41]. On the basis of the Kubelka-Munk Equation: $(\mathsf{a h v})^{2}=\mathsf{A}(\mathsf{h v}-\mathsf{E}_{\mathrm{g}})$ ( $\mathfrak{a}$ refers to the absorption coefficient, hm is the photon energy, $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the band gap, and A is a constant), $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOFs are regarded as the indirect semiconductor [33,42,43]. The band gap of Zr-MOFs was estimated to be 2.66 and $3.89\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and UiO-66, respectively (Fig. 4d), which were in accordance with the previous report [33,42,43]. However, Au and Pt loading scarcely decreased the band gap and just accompanied with the color change, previous reports also proved it [6,18]. The difference between Pt and Au loading on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs was that the loading of Au generated an absorption peak at around $520\mathrm{nm}$ owing to the LSPR effect under light illumination (Fig. 4c) [20]. The specific values of LUMO position of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and UiO-66 were estimated by their MottSchottky plots (Fig. 4e). Positive slop signified the n-type semiconductor and their flat band potential equals to the Fermi Level. Therefore, the values of Fermi Level for $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and UiO-66 are $-0.65$ and $-0.59\mathrm{eV}$ (intercept at the $x$ -axis), corresponding to their LUMO position of $-0.85$ and 0.79 eV vs. Ag/AgCl (equivalent to $-0.65$ and 0.59 eV vs. NHE), respectively [44,45]. In addition, the Mott Schottky plot slope of UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ is lower than that of UiO-66, demonstrating that the charge carrier concentration of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is higher than that of UiO-66 [46], which further certified the effect of amino functionalization.
The aforementioned UV–vis analyses shed light on the optical properties of these catalysts; nevertheless, they were insufficient to explain the inverse effect for aromatic alcohols photocatalytic oxidation and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction. Therefore, the lifetime of photoinduced electrons from M/Zr-MOFs were investigated by measuring the photocurrent over $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ , AUN and PUN. As shown in Fig. 5a, a fast and steady photocurrent response was detected for each light-on and light-off over the samples. The photocurrent density of UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ was lower compared to that of AUN and PUN, demonstrating that the lifetime of photo-induced electrons was increased and the recombination efficiency of electrons and holes was decreased after the Au and Pt loading. In addition, PUN possessed higher photocurrent density than that of AUN, showing that Pt loading would not restrain the electron transfer and the transfer efficiency was higher than that of Au loading.
The results of photocurrent tests were consistent with that of Cr (VI) photocatalytic reduction, but opposite to the results of aromatic alcohol photocatalytic oxidation. To further clarify the interface charge transport behavior of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ , AUN and PUN, the Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were carried out (Fig. 5b). The radius of the arc on the EIS Nyquist plot reflected the reaction rate occurring at the electrode surface. The lower arc radius of AUN and PUN with respect to UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ demonstrated a faster interfacial transfer and more effective separation of photo-induced electrons and holes occurred. These results matched well with the above-mentioned photocurrent tests. Hence, the photocurrent spectra and EIS Nyquist plots confirmed the superior separation efficiency of photo-induced electrons and holes and charge transfer after the Au and Pt loading.
For the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ , the LUMO position of UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ was $-0.65\mathrm{eV}$ vs. NHE (calculated by Fig. 4e) which was more negative than that of the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)/Cr(III)}$ position $(+0.57\mathrm{eV}$ vs. NHE at $\mathsf{p H}=6$ ) [11]. The photo-induced electrons could directly participate in the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ to $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ (Eq. (5)) and noble metal NPs loaded could promote the electrons transfer, leading to a enhanced photocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ reduction.
